    "name": "Karlu Karlu, Australia",
    "region": "Australia",
    "description": "Karlu Karlu , also known as the Devil’s Marbles, is situated in the southern half of the Northern Territory, Australia, within the expansive Barkly Region, which spans 322,713 square kilometers (124,600 square miles). The area is renowned for its striking, round boulders and cultural significance. &#8220;Karlu Karlu&#8221; translates to ‘round boulders’, a name shared among the Kaytetye, Warumungu, Warlpiri, and Alyawarra language groups. Recognized by Traditional Owners, the cultural landscape of Karlu Karlu extends well beyond the geological features most visitors associate with the site. Nearly the entire area is a registered sacred site under the Northern Territory Aboriginal Sacred Sites Act and is significant to the Kaytetye, Warumungu, Warlpiri, and Alyawarra peoples, whose ancestors have cared for this land since creation time. Its national importance is further emphasized by its listing on the Register of the National Estate for extraordinary geological formations.",
